---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { specificationDoc } from '../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="How sempods works — linked data, queries and authorization"
  description="A pod holds linked data and authorizes each caller. See how HTTP resources, SPARQL queries and optional MCP tools share that boundary."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">the model</p>
  <h1>One data space, many clients</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Start with the data. Give each thing an address, describe what it means,
    and let the pod decide what each caller can do. Apps and agents can then
    work with the same knowledge through a common interface.
  </p>

  <h2>Resources have web addresses</h2>
  <p>
    An event, task or place has a URI. Request it over HTTP and the pod returns
    the description you may read, usually as JSON-LD. Follow a link and you
    reach another resource, potentially on another pod.
  </p>
  <p>
    The identifier belongs to the data. A new client can use it without asking
    the previous app to translate its private IDs.
  </p>

  <h2>Shared vocabulary gives the data meaning</h2>
  <p>
    RDF describes things through statements and links. An event can use
    schema.org's Event, name and startDate terms. A calendar, website and
    agent can recognize the same facts and use them for different tasks.
  </p>
  <p>
    An app developer can work with JSON-LD or a graph library. The useful
    property is the shared meaning underneath those representations.
  </p>

  <h2>The pod authorizes every operation</h2>
  <p>
    The current specification uses Contexts, RDF named graphs, as its permission
    boundary. Each statement belongs to exactly one Context. Grants allow
    reading, writing or managing a Context; the pod resolves them server-side
    on each request.
  </p>
  <div class="proof">
    <h3>Same event, different depth</h3>
    <p>
      Public statements describe an event's title and date. Private statements
      hold booking notes. Both describe the same event URI. A visitor sees the
      public facts; the venue's authorized app can see more.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Context creation and deletion belong to an optional module. The broader{' '}<a href={specificationDoc('docs/vision.md')}>vision</a> also considers access
    without Context setup, with more freedom in how a pod applies policy. That
    generalization is <a href={specificationDoc('docs/proposals/data-access.md')}>proposed</a>;
    it is not the current normative contract or Kotlin 0.2 behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>Queries respect the same boundary</h2>
  <p>
    SPARQL lets a client ask about the graph directly. The server limits the
    query to readable Contexts. Naming a private graph does not give the
    caller authority to read it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current contract supports read-only SPARQL. Writes use HTTP CRUD and
    name their target Context explicitly. The <a href="/concepts/linked-data-crud">CRUD guide</a>{' '}explains the resource and write boundaries.
  </p>

  <h2>Agents use an optional client surface</h2>
  <p>
    A pod may expose MCP tools for finding resources, following their links
    and querying the graph. Those tools use the same authorization decisions.
    An agent gets the access granted to its connection.
  </p>
  <p>
    A hosted MCP service can connect one agent to several pods. Each pod still
    controls its own data. <a href="/concepts/mcp-over-pods">Read MCP over pods</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The contract leaves room for different implementations</h2>
  <p>
    The specification owns the protocol. sempods-kotlin provides one reference
    implementation and building blocks for clients and servers. Their versions
    advance independently; a Kotlin release does not release the specification.
  </p>
  <p>
    The development specification already defines requirements. Its governance
    records how changes are adopted and which guarantees apply before publication.
    Passing a repository build does not establish product conformance.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="/start">Try the model</a>{' '}
    <a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Follow the retrieval loop</a>{' '}
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">Read the development specification</a>{' '}
    <a href={specificationDoc('GOVERNANCE.md')}>Read governance</a>
  </p>
</Base>
